of all of the open-world games to come  out this year Xenoblade Chronicles X may  be the most formidable it is a truly  enormous game both in scale and scope  the towering animals and rock formations  stretching as far as the eye can see  even after as many as 60 hours X  continues to provide taller mountains to  climb and stronger opponents to topple  with no end of new challenges in sight  at the same time X is a prolonged RPG  with a predictable story and repetitive  lifeless characters you hear the same  jokes over and over again and endure  drawn-out cutscenes with little to no  emotional payoff like so many Japanese  made RPGs serious characters and moments  are undermined by the presence of a cute  and cuddly sidekick we aren't wincing at  the sight of Tatsu the game's stuffed  animal choice you may be reeling from  the soundtrack instead which is filled  with low-rent flops that make you reach  for the mute button and nothing is more  frustrating than when your success has  left a chance a sin that occurs an ex  more often than should these are reasons  enough to walk away from most games but  X isn't most games by offering a steady  stream of challenges and greater rewards  not to mention fantastic landscapes to  explore X has no problem enticing you  back for more despite its problems  your journey begins when you wake up  after being discovered in a Life pod on  planet mira a spaceship escaping earth  crash-landed on the planet after getting  caught up in an alien conflict and a few  humans that remain are left to fight for  their survival in the future of the  species as a member of blade the  military organization in charge of  scouring mira and defending humanity  your only goal is to locate the  invaluable pod known as the life hold  all housed in a structure called the  life during your search you travel  across alien lands and have to defend  yourself from hostile wildlife you and a  party of three ai-controlled characters  battle in a real-time combat system that  offers a lot of depth but unfortunately  like many of x's finer points you're  left to figure out how it all works on  your own every character has a melee in  a ranged weapon and depending on their  class a selection of offensive and  defensive skills at their disposal  now making use of these isn't difficult  but shortly after starting you soon  realized that not everything is under  your control instead of casting healing  spells are using consumable items to  patch up wounded warriors you rely on a  QTE system which is triggered by a  complex array of events in battle to  replenish your health sadly like many of  its advanced systems access and do a  good job of explaining how this works  something that is basic is healing in a  game what combat is central to the  experience should really be made clear  from the start  X does a poor job of explaining things  in general and you will spend a lot of  time with the in-game manual poring over  pages in search of information X allows  you to dive deep into character  development and resource management but  without guidance you will undoubtedly  get lost and sidelined more advanced  pursuits in order to continue exploring  and fighting if only because those  activities come so naturally you should  take the time to figure these subsystems  out though because they are the key to  unlocking new skills and tools that  allow you to continue hunting larger  creatures and reaping greater rewards  skills the game's giant robots are the  biggest reward of all after dozens of  hours exploring a massive planet on foot  the moment you jump into a pilot seat of  a skel is an awakening you are  considerably more powerful in combat and  when you transform into the scales  vehicle form you can cover large swaths  of land in no time it can take 30 hours  to get your first scale and that long  journey makes you truly appreciate your  new powers when they finally arrive with  multiple scales to purchase and dozens  of equipment to customize an upgrade you  will spend a lot of time filling out  your repertoire of scales but in order  to get to that point you have to  complete roughly half of X's main story  while the vagary surrounding X's  subsystems can be frustrating for  ambitious explorers  nothing is more confounding than X's  mission structure sometimes you're  required to arbitrarily explore a  certain amount of land or collect a  certain number of items in order to  access the next chapter of a story X  already has so many things for you to do  but the last thing it needs is missions  that Pat out the story unnecessarily you  often don't have enough time to do the  things you'd like to do let alone enough  time to flounder around completing  random tasks just to move to the next  part of the plot  Mira and its inhabitants are all  inspiring and experiencing everything X  has to offer is a monumental but  ultimately rewarding task it makes the  journey consistently interesting in  other ways by giving you intricate  control over your characters abilities  in gear unleashing created opportunities  over time you will roll your eyes at the  story and characters and beat your head  against the wall when you're forced to  mindlessly grind for the sake of patting  out the game's story but these  frustrations are quickly forgotten and  you head into the wilderness in search  of unexplored territory and unforeseen  challenges X is a great adventure that  sea sheets your appetite for exploration  in combat in ways that few games ever do  but because getting started is half the  battle it's an experience reserved for  dedicated players who have the patience  and energy to unearth its greatest  treasures  you
 

